    "summary": "Janis‑Joplin duet on Lovelight, first Dire Wolf live, classic Dark Star",
    "blurb": "The show features Janis Joplin’s surprise appearance on Turn On Your Lovelight and the band’s debut of Dire Wolf live. Highlights include a fiery Dark Star > The Eleven stretch and an extended Doin’ That Rag jam, with Jerry delivering nimble solos throughout.",
    "review": "On June 7, 1969 at Fillmore West the Dead delivered one of their most memorable summer outings, bolstered by an unforgettable guest spot from Janis Joplin. The set rolls from acoustic‑flavored numbers into a high‑energy second half where Dark Star segues into Saint Stephen and then explodes into The Eleven, showcasing the classic ’69 fire that defined this era. A historic moment arrives when Tom Constanten’s organ underpins Janis’s raw, soulful take on Turn On Your Lovelight – a performance many fans cite as a highlight of any Joplin‑Dead crossover. The band also cracks open an extended Doin’ That Rag jam and drops the first ever live rendition of Dire Wolf, while Jerry’s fresh, nimble soloing shines on Me And My Uncle and throughout the Lovelight duet. Two soundboard sources exist: the Kaplan tape, though hissy and low‑fidelity, captures every vocal nuance of Janis and the full set flow; the Wise reel offers cleaner audio but lacks some of the intimate moments. For listeners seeking the complete experience – especially Janis’s guest spot – the Kaplan recording remains the definitive choice.",
